WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.440
Over nearly ten years of making
weekly videos for this channel,

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:04.720
I've made some mistakes.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:05.680
When they're significant,

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.200
I will pin a comment to the video,
I'll add a card,

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:11.320
and I'll make a note on the
corrections page on my web site.

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:14.320
But not many people will see those
compared to the original video.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:19.040
So with only a few weeks left until I
stop posting regular Monday videos,

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:20.840
I figure it's time to
officially look back

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:24.160
at all the things I got wrong
in the last ten years.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:27.200
And all these mistakes
are sponsored by NordVPN!

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.080
Which I have no regrets about using
and whose Black Friday sale is now on.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.520
So: what counts as a significant
mistake for this video? Well,

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:38.800
it has to affect the truth
of what I said.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:40.840
An editing glitch,
or a typo in a caption,

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:43.280
or a mispronunciation:
they don't count.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.160
They're still going to haunt me
for the rest of my days,

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:46.440
but they happen.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:49.920
And it has to be a regular Monday video
that's live on this channel:

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.480
if it's a mistake in another show,
it doesn't count.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:55.720
I am sure some of the facts
on Lateral, the podcast I run,

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:57.320
will turn out to
be wrong in future,

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:00.120
despite the question writers'
best efforts, but here,

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:03.200
we're dealing with this series
and this channel.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.800
You should go and listen
to Lateral after, though.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:07.920
I hated writing this new script,
by the way.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.040
I despise anything I've
made in the past,

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:12.040
this was not pleasant
to look back on,

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:13.760
particularly my early stuff.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:15.560
But that's where we start.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:20.000
2014, when my research usually involved
typing a couple of things into Google

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:21.800
to check that I'd remembered
them properly.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:25.440
"The UK has four Trident-class
nuclear submarines."

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.760
Yes, I looked younger ten years ago.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:29.320
Never mind, it'll happen
to you as well.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:31.080
And the alternative's worse.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:32.960
Anyway. Trident is the
nuclear missile:

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:34.840
the submarines are
Vanguard-class.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:35.840
This is where I
should have learned

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:37.440
to check every little
fact in the video,

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:38.280
not just the big one,

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.240
but I still screw
that up sometimes.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:42.720
I hate that video.
I nearly pulled it entirely.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.920
"...tachycardia, which is
irregular heartbeat."

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.920
Nope, tachycardia is
an accelerated heartbeat.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:49.400
Again, I thought I knew a word,

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:50.760
and so I didn't check it.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:53.680
Also, hold the phone
further from your face!

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:56.320
"What these have is a
bi-metallic strip."

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.680
Modern toasters don't use
bi-metallic strips,

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:01.200
they either use variable resistors
charged by capacitors,

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:02.840
or specialised silicon.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:06.200
Alec over at Technology Connections
has a great video about them.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.920
Ironically, my video was meant
to correct the internet,

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:11.560
and I got a
big detail in it wrong.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:12.560
Now, in 2015,

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:14.080
I went to Svalbard,
and I talked about

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:16.880
the 24-hour sundial.
There are several places

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:19.360
I went to early in
"Things You Might Not Know"

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.760
and I wish I'd
saved them for later.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.400
And that's definitely
one of those places.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:24.760
Anyway, I said...

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:27.200
"It is the summer equinox today,
as I record this."

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:30.200
I said summer equinox.
It's the summer solstice.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:31.760
The equinox is in
spring and autumn,

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.480
when the days and nights
are almost equal length.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.400
It's in the name?!
"Equi nox", "equal night"

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.720
Yes, I know it's more
technical than that...

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:39.800
I don't care!

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.040
Later that year, I was
talking about Concorde.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.375
Again, not a good video, but...

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:53.320
That's a really subtle mistake,
and it's in the timing.

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:55.080
The words are correct,

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.000
but I basically moved a
comma in my head,

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.360
and then in the subtitles.

00:02:58.360 --> 00:02:59.760
The crash was in 2000,

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:02.360
the airlines decommissioned
Concorde in 2003.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:03.320
I should have said:

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:05.680
"after one of the planes crashed...

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:08.000
"...in 2003 the airlines decided".

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.040
Actually, I should have just
rewritten the whole section.

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:13.480
Next video, about tracking nuclear explosions
from Cold War bunkers.

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:15.720
"...ground bursts that
produce local fallout,

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:17.760
"air bursts that spread it
much wider?"

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:18.760
Wrong way round!

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:22.360
Ground bursts from nuclear explosions
spread more fallout than air bursts,

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:24.520
and they spread it wider,
which makes sense,

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:27.720
given they're blowing up
the ground and not the air.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.360
Next up, the St Francis Dam
near Los Angeles.

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.960
"More than 50 metres high,
holding back 100,000 tonnes of water."

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:36.560
The St Francis dam actually
held back not 100,000,

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:39.240
but 47 million tons of water.

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:40.920
I mistook the volume
of the physical dam

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:43.280
for the volume of the
reservoir it held back.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:46.560
In my defence, volume calculations
are really unintuitive.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.000
2016, then.
The year with the most errors.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:50.400
And we start with a great one:

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:55.320
"In the original version of the video game,
Civilisation, Gandhi was a dick."

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:56.920
Just to be clear, I am talking about

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:01.360
the virtual representation of Gandhi
in a fictional video game!

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:02.840
That story was meant
to be true at the time,

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:04.320
you could find loads of articles

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:06.720
testifying that a bug in
the game Civilisation

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:09.160
meant that the virtual Gandhi's
score for aggression

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:12.520
could go negative and then
loop round to being massive.

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:14.680
But years after I
put that video out:

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:17.200
turns out it was some giant
collective false memory.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:18.680
That bug does not exist.

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:20.600
I'd never actually played the game.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:22.320
Now, I also had a whole video

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:24.920
called "Why you should
write down your goals".

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:27.040
Might be the worst video
I've ever done,

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:30.120
and I say "had", because I decided to
move it to being unlisted

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:33.040
after watching it back for this.
It's just a confusing mess,

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:35.040
tacked onto a weird
story about television

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:36.920
and based on one or
two research papers.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:38.960
And this was before the
replication crisis, too,

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:42.040
when psychology was still flush with
astonishing results

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:45.760
about how people could be subconsciously
influenced and affected by things.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:49.360
A lot of those studies have not
survived attempts to recreate them.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:52.640
Thomas Frank made a great video
arguing the counterpoint.

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:55.080
I'm not sure I'd agree
with that either, though?

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:57.840
And I suspect the answer to whether
you should write down your goals is:

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.440
eh, I dunno.
Depends on you.

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:03.520
Now, there was the time that me,
and my friends Matt and Paul,

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:07.800
drove along a road that goes through
Russia for about one kilometre,

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:10.600
and as long as you don't stop,
you don't technically enter Russia,

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:11.880
you don't need a visa.

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:13.680
2016, wouldn't do that video now.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:15.880
But as we're approaching,
Matt says:

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:17.600
"That pole..."
"Yes?"

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:20.840
"Is where they're spying from."
"Ah!" "Oh, you're right!"

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:22.360
Yeah, no, it was just a cell tower.

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:23.960
Or at least, that's what they
want us to think!

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:26.600
there is no evidence for it
being any more than that.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:29.840
That's my responsibility to check
before putting it in the video.

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:32.400
Matt had just noticed a thing,
and said it,

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:34.280
and because it felt
more like a vlog,

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:36.640
I kept it in.
My fault, not his.

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:40.200
Next one: I was talking about
turbines in thermal power plants.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:44.280
"hundreds of tonnes of steel,
rotating thousands of times a second"

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:49.120
No, they spin thousands of times
per minute, not per second. Obviously!

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:50.720
If your giant electricity turbine

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:53.680
is spinning several thousand times
a second, something is very wrong,

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:56.480
and probably on fire.
I just said the wrong word.

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:59.680
Probably because I was kinda stressed
from being on top of a wind turbine.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:02.800
Next up, the first time that I have to
correct a mistake from an interviewee.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:04.600
I'm not going to play
the actual clip back,

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:06.160
it doesn't feel fair to point out

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:09.680
someone who was kind enough to
give an interview many years ago, but:

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:13.440
I was talking to one of the administrators
of West Virginia University,

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:16.040
about their personal rapid transit system,
the PRT,

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:19.920
and he said the "PRT never shuts down".

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:20.840
Yes it does!

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:25.280
At night. On Sundays. Outside term times.
Regularly when there are mechanical failures.

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:27.160
Like, that shouldn't have made the edit.

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:29.200
I guess it was said aspirationally
or metaphorically,

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:31.800
but another word for that would be...
wrong.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:33.440
And to be clear,
that's on me.

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:35.640
It's my job to make sure that
the people I interview

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:39.200
don't end up saying
something silly in the final video.

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:42.160
Right. 2017! Things start getting
a little better here,

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:45.120
I start finding my style, we're
three years into making these videos

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:48.320
and I'm actually starting to
fact-check things properly.

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:50.840
Only one error that whole year,
but it's a doozy.

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:53.800
From the zero-gravity drop tower
in Bremen, Germany:

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:57.800
"In the first second after release,
the drop capsule goes 10 metres.

00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:00.360
"Next second, 20,
next second, 30,

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:03.800
"Next second, forty...
and then we've used up

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:05.600
"pretty much all the
height there is."

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:09.520
That is wrong. Yes, the speed increases
by 10 metres per second, per second.

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:11.720
I know it's actually 9.8 something,
but, like,

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:14.560
10 metres per second is
close enough for this.

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:18.080
But that doesn't mean it travels
10 metres in the first second,

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:19.480
'cos it's starting at zero
and speeding up.

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:24.440
It'd travel 5 metres in the first second,
then 15, then 25, and so on.

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:26.600
Two separate physicists
fact-checked that script!

00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:29.360
Zero gravity is really confusing.

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:31.000
2018, then. One error:

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:34.480
"in the 20th century, more than
200,000 tons of gold

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:36.600
"were extracted from the rock
underneath here."

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:41.080
Giant Mine in Canada produced
200,000 kilos of gold. Not tonnes.

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:43.880
They also produced 200,000 tonnes
of arsenic trioxide dust,

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:45.360
which is the problem
they now have.

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:47.760
You can see how I got
confused with those numbers.

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:50.160
200,000 tonnes of gold would be
roughly the same amount

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:53.440
that the entire world has mined
throughout history,

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:55.800
so, you know,
just slightly out there.

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:59.640
2019. Two weird mistakes in
the same computer-science script.

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:02.480
"If I want to run it on a completely
different type of computer,

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:07.040
"a Mac instead of a PC, I can compile
it for that CPU instead."

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:09.520
Nope, I meant "phone" instead of a PC.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:11.960
Although given the recent switch
to Apple Silicon,

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:13.440
that's probably now true?

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:17.960
"And we're counting upwards by one
on every sixth clock cycle"

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:20.640
That's every ninth cycle,
it's obvious from the graphics.

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:23.920
And I just said the wrong words
and didn't notice during the edit.

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:27.120
2020. I know that I said typos
in on-screen graphics don't count,

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:29.240
but when you get the
speed of light wrong

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:32.680
because you copied-and-pasted
a 1 into the wrong place,

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:33.800
yeah, it counts.

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:35.560
The other error is
more interesting, though.

00:08:35.560 --> 00:08:36.680
I was talking about the rules

00:08:36.680 --> 00:08:39.680
on where British programmes
can break for adverts.

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:43.120
"And the host says..."
"Time for a break."

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:47.200
"Like, it's really clear that's not allowed,
there's no other way to read that sentence."

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:49.200
That was true...

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:52.760
back 20+ years ago when the original
version of Millionaire came out.

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:54.240
But in 2008, the rules were changed,

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:56.600
and it's now ambiguous
whether that's allowed.

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:58.920
The old broadcasting code was
still on the regulator's site,

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:00.440
it wasn't flagged as being replaced,

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:04.880
and I just didn't spot that I was using
the old version in my script.

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:09.000
2021. Skipping over the 18th-century
firefighters video that I did a

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:12.080
long correction for last year,
we have two blunders:

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:17.080
"Even the big lithium-ion setup in Australia
that's only caught on fire once so far."

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:20.520
Just like that line years earlier
about Trident-class submarines,

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:23.840
I just thought I knew what I was talking
about and I didn't double-check it.

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:25.120
The graphics are wrong,

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:27.880
Hornsdale Power Reserve
didn't catch fire!

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:31.240
It was the newer Victorian Big Battery,
near Geelong.

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:33.640
Complete blunder on my part,
sorry to the Hornsdale team.

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:35.760
Your battery did not catch on fire!

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:38.600
And then a translation mistake:
"Schusswaffen", firearms,

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:41.920
was mistakenly transcribed as
"Schutzwaffen", defensive weapons.

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:44.760
My transcriber and translator
was German...

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:47.520
...but not Swiss German, and that's
a pretty easy mistake to make.

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:50.080
Honestly I'm not sure I got it
the right way round in this video.

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:52.720
2022. No errors that are
still online,

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:55.160
but in this video about
a chainmail box surrounding a house,

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:58.440
I did originally suggest that the
chainmail was all hand-linked.

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:02.160
The sheets were hand-linked, but
those sheets were made by machine.

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:04.400
The correction's still up, but for once
it was a very brief line

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:07.400
that could be easily and cleanly
cropped out using YouTube's editor,

00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:09.600
so I just did that.

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:13.200
And then, this year, at least
this year so far!

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:16.960
Jan Antonín Baťa’s birth year
is 1898, not 1989.

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:18.960
You can see exactly
how that happened.

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:21.640
And the recent video from
the British library said that...

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:24.440
"if you want to get a library card here,
there will be a short interview

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:28.360
"about your research and why you can't
just pull the books or material you want

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:30.320
"from your local or university library."

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:33.120
Apparently the formal interview
requirement for a reader pass,

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:35.120
which I had to go through
a few years ago,

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:37.440
has now been dropped.

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:40.440
There will no doubt be more mistakes.
There's probably one in this video,

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:43.360
because that would be ironic,
and it's probably going to happen.

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:46.680
And when that happens, please:
remember Hanlon's Razor,

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:49.680
which says, in short:
"cock-up before conspiracy",

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:54.440
you should never attribute to malice
what can be adequately explained by stupidity.

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:56.760
As for what has been useful for my research?

00:10:56.760 --> 00:10:59.040
Well, NordVPN’s Black Friday deal is on!

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:01.000
I really do use them
a lot while I'm travelling,

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:03.480
because being able to tap
NordVPN’s magic button

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:05.080
and pretend that my computer and phone

